        q: "Should I charge per push or a seasonal flat rate for snow removal?",
        a: "It's a question of who carries the weather risk. Per-push puts it on the client — you get paid for every event, so a heavy winter pays well and a light one pays little. A seasonal flat rate puts the risk on you: the client buys budget certainty and you win a mild year and lose a severe one. Property managers prefer seasonal because it's a predictable budget line, which is why it commands a premium, but only price it off a five- to ten-year snowfall average for your market — then check the worst year in that window and ask whether you could survive it at that price. Many contractors settle on the middle option: per-push or tiered billing with a stated cap on billable events or a seasonal maximum, so both sides' downside is bounded.",
